1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,153
Production and testing both have a table called delivery_summary.

2
00:00:04,153 --> 00:00:07,445
A database scan and a scheduler describe that name.

3
00:00:07,445 --> 00:00:10,480
Is the matching table name enough to join their claims?

4
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:12,275
Pause and explain what else you need.

5
00:00:17,125 --> 00:00:20,008
Distinguish the environment, database and table.

6
00:00:20,008 --> 00:00:22,979
The catalogue uses prod/parcels/delivery_summary

7
00:00:22,979 --> 00:00:25,933
for production and a separate test identifier.

8
00:00:25,933 --> 00:00:28,744
Sources must agree which dataset they mean,

9
00:00:28,744 --> 00:00:31,261
recording a correspondence when names differ.

10
00:00:31,261 --> 00:00:35,941
A reused name may identify an unrelated replacement, so check identity before

11
00:00:35,941 --> 00:00:37,203
attaching old explanations.

12
00:00:38,337 --> 00:00:41,133
Now assemble the page's invented catalogue entry.

13
00:00:41,133 --> 00:00:44,758
The database scan establishes that a percentage column exists.

14
00:00:44,758 --> 00:00:48,216
The scheduler reports which run published an output version.

15
00:00:48,216 --> 00:00:52,419
Service Analytics supplies the intended completed-delivery definition.

16
00:00:52,419 --> 00:00:55,935
These are separate claims about the same production dataset.

17
00:00:55,935 --> 00:01:00,827
An output version identifies one result; it does not replace the dataset identifier.

18
00:01:01,833 --> 00:01:03,299
Look at the scan's times.

19
00:01:03,299 --> 00:01:06,079
It observed the column at nine in the morning; the

20
00:01:06,079 --> 00:01:08,858
catalogue received the record at two minutes past nine.

21
00:01:08,858 --> 00:01:10,860
When was the column created?

22
00:01:10,860 --> 00:01:13,772
Explain what those two times can establish before continuing.

23
00:01:18,722 --> 00:01:22,833
Its creation time is unknown unless the source supplies that evidence.

24
00:01:22,833 --> 00:01:27,718
Nine is the observation time; two minutes past nine is collection time.

25
00:01:27,718 --> 00:01:32,135
For the scheduler record, completion occurred at five minutes past nine and collection

26
00:01:32,135 --> 00:01:33,875
followed a minute later.

27
00:01:33,875 --> 00:01:37,266
Arrival at the catalogue does not change when the source event occurred.

28
00:01:38,184 --> 00:01:40,568
Keep each source attached to its claim.

29
00:01:40,568 --> 00:01:43,799
The scan cannot identify the run that wrote the values.

30
00:01:43,799 --> 00:01:48,317
The definition explains intended meaning, but does not prove the calculation follows

31
00:01:48,317 --> 00:01:51,333
it; inspect the run's inputs and code for that.

32
00:01:51,333 --> 00:01:55,074
The last record collected is not automatically an authority for every fact.

33
00:01:56,082 --> 00:01:58,637
Keep responsibility explicit too.

34
00:01:58,637 --> 00:02:02,388
Service Analytics accepted responsibility in its definition record.

35
00:02:02,388 --> 00:02:06,518
Importing a team name alone does not establish that agreement or make the collector's

36
00:02:06,518 --> 00:02:08,882
operator responsible for the metric's meaning.

37
00:02:08,882 --> 00:02:13,017
Collecting descriptions brings evidence together; it does not settle every question.

38
00:02:14,253 --> 00:02:16,232
Try the page's map exercise.

39
00:02:16,232 --> 00:02:19,185
In Source observation's Example record, find the

40
00:02:19,185 --> 00:02:23,056
dataset, source, observation time and collection time.

41
00:02:23,056 --> 00:02:25,654
Follow Collection run to check its scope.

42
00:02:25,654 --> 00:02:29,318
Then inspect Run: it belongs to a different day's incident.

43
00:02:29,318 --> 00:02:33,164
Those examples cannot be joined just because nearby cards look related.

44
00:02:33,164 --> 00:02:35,968
Explain the scan's limits, then use Check your answer.
